{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":370902,"duration_api_ms":364240,"num_turns":46,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"d97d0266-ad0e-4556-8dee-4adfa214be2a","total_cost_usd":2.1329545,"usage":{"input_tokens":31,"cache_creation_input_tokens":90936,"cache_read_input_tokens":2350999,"output_tokens":15558,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":90936,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":31,"outputTokens":15558,"cacheReadInputTokens":2350999,"cacheCreationInputTokens":90936,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":2.1329545,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"The `TankSnapshot` struct (snapshot.rs) does not expose `reserve_g` or `daily_food_consumed_g`. The acceptance criteria state \"those deltas are visible in budget/diagnostic outputs.\" While the budget ledger does track `animal.reserve_g` as a component, the user-facing snapshot—used by the TUI and API—omits both the reserve pool and feeding rate. Add `shrimp_reserve_g: f64` and optionally `shrimp_daily_food_consumed_g: f64` to `TankSnapshot` so the feeding pathway is inspectable without enabling the full budget ledger.","rationale":"The acceptance criteria require that \"TAN, detritus, O2 demand, and DIC... are visible in budget/diagnostic outputs.\" Reserve and feeding rate are the two new diagnostic quantities introduced by this bead that are not visible in the normal output path. The budget ledger covers conservation testing but is not enabled in normal runs, so an operator has no way to observe the retained share or feeding rate without enabling debug-level tracking."},{"body":"In `shrimp_feeding` (shrimp.rs:140-153), periphyton demand and detritus demand are computed independently at the full feeding rate, allowing shrimp to consume up to 2x the parameterized `shrimp_periphyton_grazing_g_per_shrimp_per_day` when both food sources are plentiful. This is undocumented and may cause unexpectedly high bioload. Add a comment explaining this is intentional opportunistic feeding, OR split the demand so total intake is bounded by the rate parameter (e.g., remaining demand after periphyton goes to detritus).","rationale":"The parameter name `shrimp_periphyton_grazing_g_per_shrimp_per_day` suggests it is the total grazing rate, but the code uses it as the rate for each food source independently. This double-counting means the effective feeding rate is up to 2x the named parameter, which will confuse anyone tuning the model. The mass balance is still correct, but the parameterization is misleading. At minimum a doc comment is needed; ideally the demand should be split so the parameter means what it says."},{"body":"There is no test that exercises the DO-limited respiration fallback path in `route_consumed_food` (shrimp.rs:222-256). When `dissolved_oxygen_mg_total` is insufficient to support the full respiration demand, the unrespired C and N are shunted to reserve. Add a test that sets DO to a very low level and verifies: (1) O2 does not go negative, (2) DIC increase is proportionally reduced, (3) reserve gains the overflow, and (4) total N and C are still conserved.","rationale":"The DO-limiting logic (lines 227-236) is the most complex branching path in the routing code and the most likely place for a conservation bug. The existing tests use a state with ample DO (8 mg/L × volume), so the `respiration_scale` is always ~1.0 and the overflow path to reserve is never exercised. A dedicated low-DO test would guard against future regressions in this branch."}],"summary":"The shrimp ingestion→assimilation→excretion→feces→respiration loop is well-implemented. Mass conservation is algebraically sound: the N:C ratio is maintained through all routing branches, fecal/reserve conversions between elemental mg and organic-matter grams are correct, and the budget components fully cover the new `reserve_g` pool. Parameter validation in invariants.rs correctly enforces open-unit-interval for assimilation efficiency, unit-interval for sub-fractions, positivity for O2:C ratio, and sum-to-one for the assimilated partition. Save/load migration v3→v4 correctly seeds reserve from legacy biomass. All 8 shrimp_feeding_pathway tests and 18 budget_tracking tests pass; the full test suite (100+ tests) passes cleanly with no clippy warnings. Three issues found: (1) `TankSnapshot` omits the new `reserve_g` and feeding rate from user-facing output, (2) food demand is applied independently to both periphyton and detritus yielding up to 2x the parameterized rate (undocumented), and (3) the DO-limited respiration fallback path has no dedicated test coverage."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"3379ec44-4a48-4bd5-aa2c-6c9b7bb93cca"}
